<li>Build accountability for cloud spend across teams</li>
</ul></div>

<section class="section" id="overview">
<h2>The Economics of ML on AWS</h2>
<p>Cloud cost management is often neglected until bills arrive unexpectedly high. AI/ML workloads present unique challenges: training jobs consume expensive GPU resources, inference endpoints run continuously, data storage scales linearly with datasets. Without systematic cost management, expenses can easily exceed budgets by 2-3x.</p>

<p>The goal isn't to minimize cost at all expense—it's to optimize for value. A $500/month system generating $10K revenue is efficient. The same system generating $100 revenue is wasteful. Cost optimization requires understanding what drives value and removing everything else.</p>

<div class="callout tip">
<strong>Critical Insight:</strong> Most AI/ML cost optimization opportunities are architectural, not operational. Switching instance types saves 10-20%. Rethinking architecture (batch vs. real-time inference, training frequency, data retention) saves 50-70%.
</div>

</section>

<section class="section" id="cost-structure">
<h2>Understanding AWS Cost Structure</h2>

<h3>Compute Costs</h3>
<p>Compute represents 40-50% of typical ML cloud budgets. Cost drivers: instance type (GPU instances are 5-20x more expensive than CPU), running time, scaling behavior, regional pricing variations.</p>

<p><strong>SageMaker Training:</strong> Billed per second for compute resources (no partial-minute billing). ml.p3.2xlarge (GPU) costs ~$3.06/hour. Training a model for 4 hours costs $12.24. Same training on ml.m5.xlarge (CPU) costs $0.50/hour, total $2. GPU needed? Only if speedup justifies cost.</p>

<p><strong>SageMaker Endpoints:</strong> Billed hourly whether traffic exists or not. ml.m5.large endpoint costs $0.115/hour = ~$84/month continuously. For bursty traffic, auto-scaling or Lambda may be more cost-effective. Serverless inference (SageMaker Serverless) charges per invocation, ideal for variable load.</p>

<p><strong>Lambda:</strong> $0.20 per million requests plus compute time. Handling 10M requests monthly with 1-second duration (512MB): ~$2 invocation cost + $83 compute time = $85/month. EC2 instance costs ~$50/month—Lambda preferable for variable traffic, EC2 for consistent load.</p>

<h3>Storage Costs</h3>
<p>Storage represents 20-30% of budgets for data-intensive workloads. Key factors: data volume, storage class, request frequency.</p>

<p><strong>S3 Standard:</strong> $0.023/GB/month storage plus $0.0004 per PUT, $0.0004 per GET. 100GB Standard costs $2.30/month storage. For 10K daily access requests: $0.0004 × 10K × 30 = $1.20/month. Total ~$3.50.</p>

<p><strong>Optimization:</strong> Move cold data (not accessed in 30 days) to Standard-IA ($0.0125/GB + retrieval charges). Move to Glacier (minimum 90-day retention) for archival. A dataset with active portion (10GB Standard) plus archive (90GB Glacier) costs $0.23 + $0.36 = $0.59/month vs. $2.30 all Standard.</p>

<h3>Data Transfer Costs</h3>
<p>Data transfer out of AWS costs $0.02 per GB. Often overlooked, but compounds quickly. Training a model that reads 100GB from S3: no egress cost (internal). Sending 10GB predictions to client: $0.20. For 1M daily predictions (10GB): $200/day = $6,000/month.</p>

<p><strong>Optimization:</strong> Process data within AWS where possible. Use S3 access points and CloudFront for caching. Compress outputs before transfer.</p>

<h3>API Costs</h3>
<p>Bedrock charges per token. Claude 3 Sonnet: ~$0.003 per 1K input tokens, $0.015 per 1K output tokens. Processing 1M tokens input + 500K output daily costs ~$3 + $7.50 = $10.50/day = $315/month.</p>

<p><strong>Optimization:</strong> Cache responses, compress prompts, use cheaper models (Haiku costs ~50% less than Sonnet) for simple tasks, batch requests during off-peak hours.</p>

</section>

<section class="section" id="monitoring">
<h2>Cost Monitoring and Accountability</h2>

<h3>Cost Explorer Setup</h3>
<p>Enable AWS Cost Explorer to track spending by service, resource, and tag. Set up daily alerts when spending exceeds thresholds. Create dashboards showing cost trends, service breakdown, and cost per unit of business value.</p>

<div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">boto3</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> <span class="nm">datetime</span> <span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">datetime</span>, <span class="nm">timedelta</span>

<span class="va">ce</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">boto3</span>.<span class="fn">client</span>(<span class="st">'ce'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Get last 30 days of cost by service</span>
<span class="va">end_date</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">datetime</span>.<span class="fn">now</span>().<span class="fn">date</span>()
<span class="va">start_date</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">end_date</span> <span class="op">-</span> <span class="nm">timedelta</span>(<span class="st">days</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">30</span>)

<span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">ce</span>.<span class="fn">get_cost_and_usage</span>(
    <span class="st">TimePeriod</span><span class="op">=</span>{
        <span class="st">'Start'</span>: <span class="va">start_date</span>.<span class="fn">isoformat</span>(),
        <span class="st">'End'</span>: <span class="va">end_date</span>.<span class="fn">isoformat</span>()
    },
    <span class="st">Granularity</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">'DAILY'</span>,
    <span class="st">Metrics</span><span class="op">=</span>[<span class="st">'UnblendedCost'</span>],
    <span class="st">GroupBy</span><span class="op">=</span>[{<span class="st">'Type'</span>: <span class="st">'DIMENSION'</span>, <span class="st">'Key'</span>: <span class="st">'SERVICE'</span>}]
)

<span class="cm"># Process costs by service</span>
<span class="va">costs_by_service</span> <span class="op">=</span> {}
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">result</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">response</span>[<span class="st">'ResultsByTime'</span>]:
    <span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">group</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">result</span>[<span class="st">'Groups'</span>]:
        <span class="va">service</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">group</span>[<span class="st">'Keys'</span>][<span class="nm">0</span>]
        <span class="va">cost</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">float</span>(<span class="va">group</span>[<span class="st">'Metrics'</span>][<span class="st">'UnblendedCost'</span>][<span class="st">'Amount'</span>])
        <span class="va">costs_by_service</span>[<span class="va">service</span>] <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">costs_by_service</span>.<span class="fn">get</span>(<span class="va">service</span>, <span class="nm">0</span>) <span class="op">+</span> <span class="va">cost</span>

<span class="cm"># Sort and print</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">service</span>, <span class="va">cost</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="fn">sorted</span>(<span class="va">costs_by_service</span>.<span class="fn">items</span>(), <span class="kw">key</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="kw">lambda</span> <span class="va">x</span>: <span class="va">x</span>[<span class="nm">1</span>], <span class="st">reverse</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="bi">True</span>):
    <span class="fn">print</span>(<span class="st">f"{service}: ${cost:.2f}"</span>)</code></pre>

<h3>Tagging Strategy</h3>
<p>Tag all resources with business context: project, team, environment, cost center. This enables accurate cost allocation and accountability. Common tags: Project (e.g., "ChatbotV2"), Environment (Dev/Staging/Prod), Team (DataScience/Engineering), CostCenter (billing code).</p>

<h3>Budget Alerts</h3>
<p>Set budgets and alerts in AWS Budgets service. Alert when spending exceeds threshold (80%, 100%, 120%). Action trigger can send SNS notification or trigger Lambda to auto-scale or disable resources.</p>

</section>

<section class="section" id="optimization-strategies">
<h2>Optimization Strategies by Component</h2>

<h3>Training Cost Reduction</h3>
<p><strong>Strategy 1: Spot Instances.</strong> AWS Spot provides 70% discount on compute. Downside: instances can be interrupted. Use for fault-tolerant training (distributed training with checkpointing). SageMaker Managed Spot Training automatically handles checkpointing.</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 2: Smaller Models.</strong> Smaller models train faster, cheaper. DistilBERT (66M parameters) trains 2-3x faster than BERT (110M) with minimal accuracy loss. GPT-2 (117M) vs. GPT-3 (175B)—vastly different compute requirements. Evaluate trade-off between model quality and training cost.</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 3: Batch Training.</strong> Train weekly instead of daily. Amortize infrastructure costs. Reduces flexibility but lowers expenses significantly.</p>

<p><strong>Cost example:</strong> Training daily on ml.p3.2xlarge ($3.06/hour × 4 hours × 30 days): $3,672/month. Weekly batch on same hardware: $918/month. Switching to Spot (70% discount): $276/month. Smaller model + Spot: $138/month. Trade-offs exist, but massive savings possible.</p>

<h3>Inference Cost Reduction</h3>
<p><strong>Strategy 1: Model Quantization.</strong> Reduce model size (float32 to int8): 4x smaller, faster inference, cheaper endpoint. Minor accuracy loss (typically < 1%). Especially effective for large language models.</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 2: Caching.</strong> Cache predictions for common queries (embedding lookups, common questions). Cache hit rate above 50% reduces effective inference cost 50%+.</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 3: Serverless Options.</strong> Lambda for variable traffic (pay per request). SageMaker Serverless for ML endpoints (auto-scales to zero). More expensive per request but cheaper for bursty workloads.</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 4: Model Selection.</strong> Haiku is faster and cheaper than Sonnet. Evaluate if Haiku's performance is sufficient for your use case. Mismatched models waste money.</p>

<div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
<pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="nm">boto3</span>

<span class="va">bedrock</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="nm">boto3</span>.<span class="fn">client</span>(<span class="st">'bedrock-runtime'</span>)

<span class="cm"># Cost-aware inference: route to cheaper model</span>
<span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">infer_cost_optimized</span>(<span class="va">prompt</span>, <span class="va">complexity_score</span>):
    <span class="cm"># Simple queries (score < 0.3) -> Haiku (cheapest)</span>
    <span class="cm"># Medium queries (0.3-0.7) -> Sonnet (balanced)</span>
    <span class="cm"># Complex queries (> 0.7) -> Opus (most capable)</span>

    <span class="kw">if</span> <span class="va">complexity_score</span> <span class="op"><</span> <span class="nm">0.3</span>:
        <span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">'anthropic.claude-3-haiku-20240307-v1:0'</span>
    <span class="kw">elif</span> <span class="va">complexity_score</span> <span class="op"><</span> <span class="nm">0.7</span>:
        <span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">'anthropic.claude-3-sonnet-20240229-v1:0'</span>
    <span class="kw">else</span>:
        <span class="va">model</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">'anthropic.claude-3-opus-20240229-v1:0'</span>

    <span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">bedrock</span>.<span class="fn">invoke_model</span>(
        <span class="st">modelId</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="va">model</span>,
        <span class="st">body</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">json</span>.<span class="fn">dumps</span>({
            <span class="st">"anthropic_version"</span>: <span class="st">"bedrock-2023-06-01"</span>,
            <span class="st">"max_tokens"</span>: <span class="nm">1024</span>,
            <span class="st">"messages"</span>: [{<span class="st">"role"</span>: <span class="st">"user"</span>, <span class="st">"content"</span>: <span class="va">prompt</span>}]
        })
    )

    <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="nm">json</span>.<span class="fn">loads</span>(<span class="va">response</span>[<span class="st">'body'</span>].<span class="fn">read</span>())</code></pre>

<h3>Storage Cost Reduction</h3>
<p><strong>Strategy 1: Compression.</strong> Parquet compression reduces storage 50-80% vs. raw CSV. GZIP compression adds another 30-50%. Typical dataset: 100GB CSV → 5GB Parquet → 1-2GB with GZIP. Storage cost drops from $2.30/month to $0.05/month.</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 2: Lifecycle Policies.</strong> Automatic transition to cheaper storage classes. Data accessed daily: Standard ($0.023/GB). Accessed monthly: Standard-IA ($0.0125/GB). Not accessed quarterly: Glacier ($0.004/GB). Saves 40-80% on archived data.</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 3: Selective Archival.</strong> Not all data needs to be accessible. Archive logs older than 90 days. Delete training datasets after model deployed. Maintain only essential data in Standard.</p>

</section>

<section class="section" id="architecture-design">
<h2>Cost-Aware Architecture Design</h2>

<h3>Batch Processing vs. Real-Time</h3>
<p>Real-time inference requires continuously running endpoints (expensive). Batch processing invokes compute on schedule, scales to zero between batches (cheaper). Cost difference: 10-50x for same inference volume depending on traffic patterns.</p>

<p><strong>When batch is viable:</strong> Daily recommendation updates, nightly model scoring, weekly reports. Users accept slightly stale predictions.</p>

<p><strong>When real-time required:</strong> Immediate predictions needed, user-facing features, latency-sensitive workloads.</p>

<h3>Managed Services vs. Self-Managed</h3>
<p>Managed services (Bedrock, SageMaker) simplify operations but cost more per unit of compute. Self-managed (EC2, Lambda) requires more work but can be cheaper at scale. Decision point: team size and operational complexity.</p>

<p><strong>Startup (< $100K monthly spend):</strong> Managed services preferred. Operational overhead isn't worth small cost savings.</p>

<p><strong>Growth (> $1M monthly spend):</strong> Self-managed infrastructure justified. Potential 30-40% cost reduction offsets operational complexity.</p>

</section>


<section class="section" id="advanced-topics">
<h2>Advanced Topics and Deep Dives</h2>

<h3>Multi-Region Data Replication Strategies</h3>
<p>For global systems, replicating data across regions ensures availability and reduces latency. AWS Cross-Region Replication (CRR) automatically copies objects to another region. Critical decisions: which regions, replication lag tolerance, cost (doubles storage).</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 1: Full Replication:</strong> All data in all regions. Highest cost but lowest latency from any region. Use for critical datasets.</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 2: Selective Replication:</strong> Replicate only critical datasets (model artifacts, frequently accessed training data). Accept latency for historical data.</p>

<p><strong>Strategy 3: Predictive Replication:</strong> Replicate data to region before it's needed (e.g., EU region on EU holidays when traffic increases).</p>

<h3>Data Lake Architecture for ML</h3>
<p>Data lakes centralize all organization data in S3. ML teams access raw data, create processed datasets, store outputs. Challenges: organizing massive data, preventing quality degradation, managing metadata, controlling costs.</p>

<p><strong>Medallion Architecture:</strong> Bronze (raw data), Silver (cleaned/processed), Gold (business-ready). Each layer has different access patterns, storage classes, retention policies. Bronze uses S3 Standard-IA (inexpensive), Gold uses Standard (high availability).</p>

<h3>Data Pipeline Orchestration</h3>
<p>ML data pipelines involve multiple steps: ingest, validate, transform, prepare, store. Orchestration tools (AWS Step Functions, Apache Airflow) manage dependencies and handle failures.</p>

<p><strong>Pipeline Design:</strong> Each step is idempotent (can be re-run safely), produces versioned outputs, has monitoring and alerting. Failed steps trigger alerts and can be retried.</p>

<h3>Cost Analysis and Optimization</h3>
<p>Track costs by dataset, by purpose, by retention period. Identify expensive datasets. Challenge: is 10TB legacy dataset worth $230/month? Archive if accessed < quarterly.</p>

<p><strong>Cost Optimization Opportunities:</strong> Move cold data to cheaper storage, delete duplicates, compress efficiently, deduplicate across versions, implement usage-based retention (auto-delete old versions).</p>

<h3>Data Quality and Validation</h3>
<p>Poor data creates poor models. Monitor data quality: distribution changes (data drift), missing values, outliers, schema violations. Automated validation catches issues early.</p>

<p><strong>Validation Approaches:</strong> Schema validation (expected columns, types), value validation (within expected ranges), distribution validation (compare to baseline), anomaly detection (statistical outliers).</p>

<h3>Metadata Management</h3>
<p>Tracking data lineage is critical: where did this dataset come from? What transformations were applied? Who produced it? When? Which datasets depend on it? Metadata answers these questions.</p>

<p><strong>Metadata Strategies:</strong> Manifests (JSON files listing dataset contents), tagging (label datasets by purpose, owner), data catalogs (searchable registry of all datasets), lineage graphs (show data dependencies).</p>
</section>


