<section class="section" id="case-studies">
<h2>Real-World Implementations</h2>

<h3>Real-World Implementation Case Studies</h3>

<p><strong>Case Study: ML Training Data Pipeline at Scale</strong></p>
<p>A computer vision company processes 100M images daily for model training. Initial approach: upload all to S3 Standard, train on all data. Problem: S3 costs $2.3M/year, training takes weeks. Solution: implemented tiered approach. Raw images in Standard-IA ($1.15M/year). Active training datasets in Standard (versioned, immutable). Old training runs archived to Glacier ($100K/year). Implemented automated lifecycle policies. Result: 50% cost reduction, faster training through better organization.</p>

<p><strong>Case Study: Multi-Region Disaster Recovery</strong></p>
<p>A financial services company required disaster recovery: RPO of 1 hour, RTO of 4 hours. Solution: enabled cross-region replication to backup region with 1-hour lag. Automated failover scripts in both regions. Tested failover monthly. Cost: 50% more storage for replicas. Benefit: survived two regional outages with minimal disruption. Worth the cost for mission-critical operations.</p>

<p><strong>Case Study: Cost Optimization Through Intelligence</strong></p>
<p>A startup's S3 bill grew from $100/month to $10K/month as they scaled. Analysis: 60% of storage was old model artifacts never accessed. Solution: implemented metadata tagging, automated deletion of artifacts older than 90 days unless explicitly tagged "keep". Deleted 50TB of dead data. Implemented archive-to-Glacier for models older than 30 days. Final bill: $2K/month (20% of peak). Same functionality, massive cost reduction through intelligent deletion.</p>

</section>

<div class="key-takeaways">
<h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cost Structure Varies Dramatically:</strong> Different services have different economics. Choose based on usage patterns, not just capability</li>
<li><strong>Architecture Decisions Drive Costs:</strong> Real-time vs. batch, managed vs. self-managed—massive cost implications</li>
<li><strong>Storage Optimization is High-Leverage:</strong> Choosing appropriate storage class reduces costs 50-80% for archived data</li>
<li><strong>Monitoring is Essential:</strong> You can't control what you don't measure. Daily cost tracking identifies anomalies quickly</li>
<li><strong>Trade-offs are Everywhere:</strong> Cheaper models, smaller models, batch processing—evaluate business impact vs. cost savings</li>
<li><strong>Spot Instances are Powerful:</strong> 70% discount justifies fault-tolerance engineering for non-critical workloads</li>
<li><strong>Culture Matters:</strong> Cost awareness across teams (not just finance) drives sustainable optimization</li>
</ul>
